dsh: reasoning:
Now I understand the task. Let me analyze the correction carefully and form a plan.

First, note the instruction: "Si detectas otra falta de definición que cambie un resultado, infórmala antes de editar." I should check whether the correction resolves all ambiguities. Let me analyze each.

The correction closes:
- AMBIGÜEDAD-1: ratified (slot(PoW)=0, d = slot of honest tip).
- AMBIGÜEDAD-2 (E1): resolved: E1 is exactly Nakamoto §11, no k, formula given with λ = z·q/p, and formula: P(z) = 1 − Σ_{k=0}^{z} (λ^k e^{-λ}/k!)·(1 − (q/p)^{z−k}). Note the standard Nakamoto formula (with double-spend attempt, rider) is:
  P = 1 − Σ_{k=0}^{z} (λ^k e^{-λ}/k!)·(1 − (q/p)^{z−k})
  where λ = z q/p. Yes, that's the standard formula from the whitepaper. Wait, the whitepaper formula is:
  P = 1 - \sum_{k=0}^{z} \frac{\lambda^k e^{-\lambda}}{k!} \left(1 - \left(\frac{q}{p}\right)^{z-k}\right)
  Yes. And the table in the whitepaper: q=0.1, q=0.3 for z=0..10. Good.
- AMBIGÜEDAD-3 (E2): resolved in the correction: each branch's own PoST phase starts at the instant its terminal exists (t_H for honest, t_A for adversary). Slot j ≥ 1 occurs at t_rama + j·r. In each slot honest gains weight 1 with prob (1−a)p, adversary with a·p. Adversary favorable assumption declared. FC-3/FC-2: wins at first t ≥ t_A with W_A(t) ≥ W_H(t) (ties adversary). Online node additionally d < F_slots, d = slot index of last PoST block of its honest tip (0 if none). Fresh node changes without C-FIN-01. Horizon M slots from t_H.

  Wait, careful: "El adversario publica en el primer instante en que su historia gana según la regla; un nodo en línea cambia si además d < F_slots, con d = índice de slot del último bloque PoST de su punta honesta (0 si aún no hay ninguno)." So success for online node = adversary wins (W_A ≥ W_H) at some time AND d < F_slots at that time. Actually, need to parse: The adversary publishes at the first instant his history wins according to the rule. An online node changes if additionally d < F_slots. And "Un nodo que sincroniza desde cero cambia sin C-FIN-01." So fresh node success = adversary wins at some point within horizon.

  Also E2 terminal: both branches have terminal exactly at height H*. Honest reaches H* at t_H; adversary at t_A (its k-th private block). If adversary hasn't reached H* when M slots passed since t_H, fails.

  Hmm, H* is a parameter? The grid doesn't include H*. Hmm. Actually the correction says "Ambas ramas tienen su terminal exactamente a la altura H*". And the adversary branches at depth k from the honest terminal, mining k blocks privately. Actually wait: "En el instante en que la cadena honesta tiene altura H* − k, el adversario empieza a minar en privado desde ese bloque." So the adversary starts mining from the block at height H*−k (the common block), and must mine k private blocks to reach height H*. The honest branch continues from H*−k to H*, needing k blocks too. So both mine k blocks in a race: honest at rate 1−h, adversary at rate h. t_H is when honest reaches H*, t_A when adversary reaches H*. This is a race of two Poisson processes. Since k is the number of blocks each needs, this is a negative-binomial race. Probability honest wins (t_H < t_A) etc. But do we actually need H*? H* itself cancels since only k matters. But TRN-04 requires height ≥ H_corte_min and work ≥ W_min; the correction says W_min doesn't bind (all blocks worth 1) and Φ true in both. So H* only needs to satisfy H* ≥ H_corte_min; it's a modeling constant. Effectively E2 depends on k, h, a, p, r, F_slots.

  Actually, wait: The terminal is the *first* block at height H* on each branch. In the honest branch the terminal at H* is the honest chain tip. In the adversary's branch, the k-th private block is at height H*. Good.

- AMBIGÜEDAD-4 (E3): resolved by abstraction: adversary rewrites last k blocks at depth k, mines k private blocks with individual work 1+δ, rate h/(1+δ). δ ∈ {0.01, 0.1}. Terminal at same height H*, prefix work H* + k·δ > H*. Adversary produces no PoST (a doesn't intervene). FC-1: wins as soon as it publishes its terminal (more work), no PoST; online changes only if d < F_slots at that instant; fresh node changes always. Report probability that online don't change and fresh do (partition) as function of (h,k,δ,F_slots,r,p). FC-3: wins only if W_A ≥ W_H; with W_A=0 wins only while W_H=0 (no honest PoST block yet). Report that probability. Cost k(1+δ).

- AMBIGÜEDAD-5 (E4): resolved: Φ true as soon as there's one mature deposit in the selected chain (S_min=q, K_min=1, SEC-0); H_corte_min and W_min don't bind. There's always a pending deposit in honest mempool from t=0. All honest blocks include pending deposits; adversary's never. A deposit included at height b matures when selected chain reaches height b + M_dep with that block inside. Adversary strategy: each time an honest block with deposit appears in selected chain, starts mining privately from its parent and tries to overtake the honest branch (publishes when its private branch is longer) before the deposit matures; if it succeeds, deposit leaves chain and returns to mempool; if deposit matures, the cut occurs. Only pursues one deposit at a time. Measure for h∈{0.1;0.25;0.4;0.5;0.6;0.9} and M_dep∈{1,3,6,12}: delay of cut (mean and p99, in T_pow) and fraction of selected-chain blocks mined by adversary until the cut (captured emission). Horizon 10^4 T_pow; if no cut, count as censored in horizon and report fraction of censored replicas (for h > 1/2 expect near 1: state it and give bound).

- AMBIGÜEDAD-6: ratified ("Tus resoluciones de AMBIGÜEDAD-1 ... y AMBIGÜEDAD-6 quedan ratificadas"). AMBIGÜEDAD-6 was: validity of a candidate without PoST suffix under FC-1. The progress note said "Se puede fijar sin bloquear si el director confirma que FC-1 solo reordena la comparación entre historias válidas (y por tanto con sufijo PoST obligatorio)". Ratified means FC-1 only compares valid histories with PoST suffix. Good. In E3, the correction explicitly says FC-1: the adversary wins as soon as it publishes its terminal with more work, no PoST needed ("gana en cuanto publica su terminal (más trabajo), sin peso PoST"). Hmm, that contradicts AMBIGUEDAD-6 ratification? Well, E3 is an explicit special-case definition. The correction overrides: E3 FC-1 adversary wins with more work without PoST. So in E3, the candidate is admissible by definition. OK.

So the correction resolves the blocking ambiguities. Are there remaining undefined things that change a result? Let me think carefully.

Potential issues:

1. **E2 t_A distribution and the honest branch PoST clock.** "Cada rama con terminal empieza su propia fase PoST en el instante en que su terminal existe (t_H para la honesta, t_A para la del adversario)." Good. But wait: the honest branch's terminal at H* — is that the honest chain tip? Actually the honest branch continues mining from H*−k; whichever block reaches H* first... Hmm, the honest terminal is at H* regardless. Both are in a race. The honest reaches H* at t_H; adversary at t_A. If t_H < t_A, honest has terminal first. Adversary might still catch up and reach H* later. Yes.

   But there's subtlety: the honest PoST phase starts at t_H, but the adversary's terminal t_A may be *before* t_H? Actually both branch from the same block at height H*−k at the same instant (call t=0). The honest needs k blocks, adversary needs k blocks. They're in a race. So either can reach H* first. If adversary reaches H* first (t_A < t_H), the adversary's PoST phase starts before the honest's. Then the adversary already has a history that "wins"? Hmm, wait — for the adversary to win per the rule, he needs W_A(t) ≥ W_H(t) at some t ≥ t_A, and additionally the honest node must have a competing history. Actually the comparison is between the adversary's branch (terminal at H*, same work) and the honest branch's selected history.

   Hmm, but important: if adversary reaches H* first, then at that moment the honest branch hasn't reached H*, so the honest selected chain is at lower height H*−k+... The online node's tip is the honest tip. "d = índice de slot del último bloque PoST de su punta honesta (0 si aún no hay ninguno)." So before honest reaches H*, honest has no PoST blocks, d=0. But also honest history at lower height has less work. Does the adversary's branch "win according to the rule"? FC-3: if any PoST suffix exists, the largest PoST weight wins. At t_A, adversary has terminal but no PoST blocks yet (W_A=0), honest has no PoST either (W_A=0=W_H). Hmm, the rule: "si hay algún sufijo PoST, gana el de más peso PoST; si no, más trabajo PoW." At t_A, no PoST suffix exists (W_A=W_H=0), so it falls back to PoW work. Adversary work = H* (terminal at H*), honest work = H*−k+something < H*. So adversary wins on PoW work at t_A? Hmm, but the correction says "FC-3 / FC-2: el adversario gana en el primer t ≥ t_A con W_A(t) ≥ W_H(t)". Since W_A(t_A)=W_H(t_A)... W_H(t_A) is 0 because honest has no PoST yet (honest terminal not reached). Hmm, but does W_H count PoST weight of the honest branch's selected chain even if it hasn't reached its terminal? Before honest reaches H*, the honest chain has no PoST blocks, so W_H=0. So W_A ≥ W_H trivially at t_A, meaning adversary wins immediately upon reaching H*. That seems to be the model: the adversary's branch wins if weights tie, and the adversary is favored.

   Hmm, but wait — is the honest branch's selected chain even valid if the honest node's chain hasn't reached H*? In this abstract model, the honest node is just mining. The rule compares the two branches. Let me not overthink; the correction gives an explicit rule: adversary wins at first t ≥ t_A with W_A(t) ≥ W_H(t). The grid parameters include k, and this race determines t_A. Fine.

   But then: what is the significance of the PoW race for the *honest* node's d? d = slot index of last PoST block of the honest tip. If honest hasn't reached terminal, honest has no PoST blocks, d=0. Hmm, but d=0 < F_slots (for F_slots ≥ 1), and F_slots ∈ {100,1000,10000,∞}. So d=0 always passes the online check. Meanwhile fresh node always changes. So then online and fresh would coincide whenever adversary wins before honest gets any PoST block. Interesting. Actually that's the intended content: the honest node's protection d < F_slots is weak early.

   Wait, but there's a subtlety: "d = índice de slot del último bloque PoST de su punta honesta (0 si aún no hay ninguno)." Actually the ordering: For E2 correction, d is recomputed at the instant of publication. Since the adversary publishes at first t with W_A ≥ W_H. So d is the honest tip's last PoST slot at that instant.

2. **E2: what if W_H=0 forever because honest never produces PoST?** Then adversary wins at t_A. Fine.

3. **E2 H* — does it matter at all?** The correction says Φ true in both, W_min doesn't bind. So H* is not needed. But for E2's terminal race, both need k blocks from the common block at H*−k. So H* cancels. Good.

4. **E2: "bloques PoW privados minados"** metric. In E2, adversary mines until it reaches H* (k blocks) plus possibly continues? Actually the terminal race determines t_A. The adversary mines k blocks. But does the adversary continue mining PoW after reaching H*? In the model, after terminal, PoST phase. The number of PoW private blocks mined = k (to reach terminal) presumably. But metric asks for private PoW blocks mined — could include blocks mined during the PoST phase? No, after the terminal there are no more PoW blocks (TRN-05). So it's k, times... Actually k is a grid parameter, so the metric is trivially k. Hmm. Wait, maybe the adversary might fail to reach H* and abandon; then blocks mined < k possibly. Actually the metric might be the total PoW work spent, which is k on success. Since k is fixed, that's trivial. Maybe the interesting metric is the number of private PoW blocks mined *before the honest reaches H***? Hmm. The order says "bloques PoW privados minados" as a metric. I'll record it as the number of adversarial PoW blocks mined up to publication (which is k if the adversary reached H*, but if honest won the race and adversary loses, it's still up to k). Actually success requires reaching H* and winning the PoST race. Hmm.

   Actually, let me reconsider. The failure condition: "Si el adversario todavía no ha llegado a H* cuando han pasado M slots desde t_H, fracasa." So M is measured from t_H. If the adversary hasn't reached H* by then, fail. But if it reaches H* after M slots, fail. So the number of private blocks mined is at most... it could keep mining? It says fails. So metric = k for successes (adversary necessarily mined k blocks to have terminal H*). I'll report it.

5. **E3: what exactly is the probability to report?** "Reporta la probabilidad de que los nodos en línea no cambien y los nuevos sí (partición entre nodos viejos y nuevos) en función de (h, k, δ, F_slots, r, p)." And FC-3: "gana solo si W_A ≥ W_H; con W_A = 0 gana únicamente mientras W_H = 0... Reporta esa probabilidad."

   For E3 FC-1: The adversary mines k private blocks at rate h/(1+δ) starting... when? Presumably from the block at depth k from the honest terminal, i.e., from the same point, "después de que exista T (reescribe los últimos k bloques)". Hmm: "El adversario bifurca a profundidad k del terminal honesto después de que exista T (reescribe los últimos k bloques)". So the honest terminal T exists (honest reached H*). The adversary rewrites the last k blocks, i.e., starts from the block at height H*−k on the honest chain, and mines k blocks with work (1+δ) each at rate h/(1+δ). Meanwhile honest continues extending its chain from H* with PoST blocks (no more PoW, since TRN-05 prohibits PoW descendants of terminal). Wait — actually the honest chain after terminal T continues with PoST blocks. The adversary is mining PoW blocks to reach height H* with more work. When it publishes, the honest node compares. FC-1: adversary's prefix work H*+kδ > H* so it wins. Online node: changes only if d < F_slots at that instant, where d is the honest tip's last PoST slot. Fresh node changes always.

   So "the probability that online don't change and fresh do" = probability that adversary publishes (reaches H*) AND d ≥ F_slots at that time. Because fresh always changes if it publishes; online changes only if d < F_slots. Wait, but I need to be careful: the adversary's publication time t_A is when it completes k blocks at rate h/(1+δ). The honest PoST phase started at t=0 (when T existed). At t_A, honest has accumulated some number of PoST blocks; d = slot index of the last honest PoST block. Actually the honest tips advance each slot. d at time t = number of the last slot ≤ t/r in which honest produced a block? Hmm, slot j occurs at t_rama + j·r. d = index of slot of last PoST block of the honest tip. If honest produced at least one block by t_A, d = slot index of the last produced block.

   So P(online doesn't change and fresh does) = P(t_A such that d(t_A) ≥ F_slots). Since fresh always changes, this is P(d(t_A) ≥ F_slots). Hmm, "reporta la probabilidad de que los nodos en línea no cambien y los nuevos sí (partición entre nodos viejos y nuevos)". Yes.

   Actually wait, there's subtlety: FC-1 "gana en cuanto publica su terminal (más trabajo), sin peso PoST". So the online node updates only if its C-FIN-01 allows: d < F_slots. If d ≥ F_slots, online node refuses. Fresh node (sync from zero) accepts. So partition probability = P(d(t_A) ≥ F_slots). Where d is the honest tip's last PoST slot. Note: if no honest PoST block yet, d=0 < F_slots (F_slots≥... well F_slots ∈ {100,...}; if F_slots could be 0? §6 test says F_slots=0 edge; then d ≥ 0 always true, online never changes). OK.

   For E3 FC-3: "gana solo si W_A ≥ W_H; con W_A = 0 gana únicamente mientras W_H = 0". Since adversary produces no PoST weight (W_A = 0), FC-3 requires W_H = 0, i.e., the honest branch has no PoST blocks yet at the moment the adversary publishes. So P = P(honest has produced no PoST block by t_A). That is, P(t_A < first honest PoST slot time).

   Hmm, but note E3's horizon: does the adversary have a horizon M? The order mentions horizon M slots for E2. For E3, "mina k bloques privados" — presumably publishes when done, no horizon. Maybe apply M? The correction doesn't mention a horizon for E3. I'll assume no horizon (or effectively the adversary always eventually publishes), and the metrics are as described. Actually, hmm, the grid has M = 10·F_slots. But E3's definition doesn't mention abandoning. I'll use no horizon for E3 (adversary eventually publishes), and compute probabilities as exact formulas. That seems cleanest and matches "gana en cuanto publica".

   Hmm, but is E3's rate h/(1+δ) with k blocks and the honest PoST phase starting at t=0. Yes.

6. **E4 details:** The correction is fairly complete. But there are subtleties that could affect results:

   - "cada vez que aparece en la cadena seleccionada un bloque honesto con depósito, empieza a minar en privado desde su padre e intenta adelantar a la rama honesta (publica cuando su rama privada es más larga) antes de que el depósito madure; si lo consigue, el depósito sale de la cadena y vuelve al mempool; si el depósito madura, el corte ocurre."

     So we simulate a chain: honest blocks arrive Poisson rate 1−h, adversary blocks rate h. Every honest block includes pending deposits. The selected chain — hmm, need to model the longest-chain race. When an honest block with a deposit is included at height b, the adversary starts mining privately from its parent (height b−1) and tries to build a longer private branch. If adversary's private branch becomes longer than the public chain, it publishes and reorgs; the deposit block is removed, deposit returns to mempool. If the selected chain reaches height b + M_dep with the deposit block still in it, the deposit matures and the cut occurs.

     Hmm, but the adversary only pursues one deposit at a time. And "cada vez que aparece en la cadena seleccionada un bloque honesto con depósito" — every honest block includes deposits until the cut. So there is always a pending deposit. Since only one is pursued at a time, after a failed attempt (deposit matures → cut) we stop. If the adversary succeeds in reorging, the deposit returns to mempool and will be included in the next honest block; then adversary starts a new attempt. So the process continues until some deposit matures.

     This is essentially the classic "selfish mining / stubborn mining to censor" but simpler: it's a race where the adversary starts a private branch from the parent of the deposit block and needs to overtake before M_dep confirmations.

   - Need precise rules for the chain simulation. Let me define:
     - Public (selected) chain: honestly mined blocks at rate 1−h; adversary's published blocks also extend it.
     - The adversary mines privately; when its private branch length exceeds the public branch length, it publishes and replaces the public branch. Actually "publica cuando su rama privada es más larga". So publish when private length > public length. On publishing, the private branch becomes the selected chain (the adversary has more work = longer). What happens to the deposit? If the reorg removes the honest deposit block, deposit returns.

     But careful: if the adversary's private branch is longer than the public chain, does the tip of the private chain include a deposit? The adversary's own blocks never include deposits, but the private branch's prefix (up to the fork point, which is the parent of the deposit block) doesn't include the deposit. So reorg removes it. Good.

   - "si el depósito madura, el corte ocurre" — when selected chain reaches height b + M_dep. At that point the deposit at height b is mature.

   - Delay of cut: measured from... "retraso del corte" — relative to what baseline? Probably the cut would occur when the first deposit matures without adversary; with adversary it's delayed. The natural baseline: from t=0, the first honest block with a deposit is at some height b; it matures after M_dep more blocks. Hmm. The correction says measure "retraso del corte (media y percentil 99, en T_pow)". Delay relative to the cut without adversary. I need to define baseline. Presumably delay = time when cut occurs minus time when it would have occurred with no adversary.

     Hmm, this is a possible remaining ambiguity. Let me think about the most natural: The "cut delay" is the time until Φ becomes true. Without adversary, the first deposit at t=0 (mempool always has one) is included in the first honest block, at height 1, and matures at height 1+M_dep. The time for the chain to reach height 1+M_dep is sum of 1+M_dep exponential(rate 1) [total rate 1−h + h = 1 if adversary mines publicly too]. Hmm, but in the model, all blocks (honest+adversary public) count. Total block rate is 1. So baseline time to height 1+M_dep is Gamma(1+M_dep, 1).

     Actually, but without an adversary, h=0, all blocks honest, deposit included in every block. First block at height 1, matures at height 1+M_dep. So delay baseline = time to mine 1+M_dep blocks at rate 1.

     With adversary, the cut occurs when some deposit matures. Delay = T_cut − T_baseline? Or just T_cut (delay of the cut itself)? "retraso del corte" = delay of the cut. I think it's T_cut as measured from t=0 (since baseline is immediate). Actually, it might be relative to the no-adversary case. I'll define both T_cut (from t=0) and note. Hmm, but the correction says "retraso del corte (media y percentil 99, en T_pow)". I'll interpret as time from t=0 to cut (the delay imposed by censorship), and additionally could report the excess over the no-adversary baseline. Let me think: the no-adversary cut time has mean 1+M_dep (unit of T_pow). So "retraso" could be E[T_cut] − (1+M_dep). I'll report T_cut mean and p99 primarily and perhaps excess.

     Hmm. I should be careful, but this is a modeling choice. Let me look at whether the correction is precise enough. It says "Mide ... retraso del corte (media y percentil 99, en T_pow) y fracción de bloques de la cadena seleccionada minados por el adversario hasta el corte (emisión capturada). Horizonte 10⁴·T_pow". So the "retraso del corte" is the time until the cut, which is the delay. I'll define it as T_cut from t=0 and clearly state the baseline. Good.

   - Fraction of selected-chain blocks mined by the adversary until the cut: count adversary blocks in the selected chain at cut time / total selected-chain blocks. This is the captured emission.

   - For h ≤ 1/2, the correction says the same strategy? "Estrategia del adversario: cada vez que aparece..." applies to all h. The original order said with h ≤ 1/2 measure delay by partial reorgs. The correction simplifies to the single strategy. So simulate same for all h. For h > 1/2 near 1 censored.

   - One subtlety: when the adversary publishes a longer private branch, does it immediately start a new attempt for the deposit that returns to mempool? The next honest block will include it, and the whole thing repeats. Also, could the adversary be mining privately for deposit #1 while a previous attempt... only one at a time, so while pursuing, no new attempt starts. On success (reorg), it starts a new attempt when the next honest deposit block appears. But wait, after publishing its longer branch, the adversary's published blocks are part of the selected chain. Then mining continues? The model of block arrival: honest blocks rate 1−h, adversary blocks rate h. When adversary mines a block, is it public or private? It depends whether it's pursuing. Hmm, this is getting complex.

     Let me carefully design a discrete-event simulation for E4:
     - We maintain a public chain (selected chain) as a list of block heights with miner identity (H or A).
     - The adversary has a private branch when pursuing: private_length (blocks above fork point), public_length_at_start etc. Actually standard: adversary mines private blocks; the public chain grows with honest blocks (and adversary blocks not part of private mining?).

     Hmm. Let me simplify to the intended race. The adversary "empieza a minar en privado desde su padre e intenta adelantar a la rama honesta (publica cuando su rama privada es más larga)". So while pursuing, the adversary dedicates all its hash to the private branch (rate h), and the honest network extends the public chain (rate 1−h). The public chain includes earlier adversary-published blocks, but those are just part of history at the fork point.

     - At attempt start: fork point at height b−1 (parent of deposit block at height b). Actually the deposit block is honest and selected; adversary starts from its parent (height b−1). The public chain currently has the deposit block at height b (tip maybe b, or could be... at the moment the honest block appears, it extends the tip to b). Actually "empieza a minar en privado desde su padre" immediately when the deposit block appears.
     - Private branch: adversary needs to mine blocks to a height > public tip height. Initially private height = b−1 (fork point). Public tip = b. Adversary mines; when its private branch reaches height ≥ b+1? Wait, "publica cuando su rama privada es más larga": private length (from fork) > public length (from fork). Fork at b−1. Public length from fork = 1 (block b). Private length = number of private blocks. Adversary publishes when private length > public length, i.e., private blocks ≥ 2 while public has only block b. But standard selfish mining: publish when private chain length exceeds public. Yes.

     Hmm, but there's a subtlety: as the honest network extends the public chain, the required private length grows. The deposit matures when public chain (with block b) reaches height b + M_dep. So if the honest chain grows to b+M_dep before the adversary's private branch exceeds the public length, the deposit matures → cut. If the adversary publishes a longer branch before that, the deposit is removed.

     Now, once the adversary publishes at height b−1+L_priv with L_priv > L_pub, does the deposit block get removed? Yes, the reorg replaces blocks b, b+1, ..., so the deposit at b is gone.

     But there's the "honest" chain after the reorg: the selected chain is now the adversary's branch, whose tip is an adversary block. Then honest mining continues from there. The pending deposit (back in mempool) gets included in the next honest block. Then a new attempt starts.

     Wait, but also the adversary might have a head start: after publishing, its private branch becomes public; the next honest block appears at rate 1−h on top of the adversary's tip, including the deposit. Then the adversary restarts private mining from that block's parent = the adversary's tip. Hmm, the deposit block's parent is the adversary's tip. So fork point = adversary tip. Then race again.

     Actually, the adversary starts mining from the parent of the deposit block — which is its own previous block. So effectively the adversary restarts from its tip. It needs to overtake the honest deposit block plus subsequent.

     Hmm, this is essentially a repeated "delete one honest block" attempt. Each attempt is: honest mines a block with deposit (rate 1−h), adversary tries to build a longer private chain from before it before M_dep more honest/public blocks build on it.

     Let me define attempt termination conditions precisely:
     - Attempt starts at the moment the deposit block is added to the public chain. Let public tip height = b (the deposit block). Private height = b−1.
     - Events: honest mines next block (rate 1−h) → public tip += 1, with deposit still at b. Adversary mines private block (rate h) → private height += 1.
     - If private height > public tip height → adversary publishes, reorg, deposit removed → attempt ends in success; next attempt begins when next honest block with deposit is added (the honest chain from the adversary's tip gets a new honest block). Actually after publishing, the selected tip is the adversary's private tip (height = private height). Then next honest block added on top → that's the new deposit block, and a new attempt starts immediately.
     - If public tip height ≥ b + M_dep → deposit matures → cut. (public tip = b+M_dep means the chain reached b+M_dep.) Ends.
