Now E4 simulator. Let me design it carefully as a discrete-event process with continuous time.

State:
- t = 0.
- selected chain: we need its current height (number of blocks) and the composition counts (adversary vs total). Actually for E4 metrics we need the selected chain length and adversary block count at cut.
- We model the "attempt" cycle:
  - The selected chain has height h_sel (total blocks). At t=0, height 0 (genesis).
  - Honest blocks arrive at rate (1−h). When an honest block arrives and there's a pending deposit not currently "protected", it includes the deposit → deposit block at height h_sel+1 = b. The selected height becomes b. Then an attempt starts.
  - During an attempt: honest blocks continue to arrive (rate 1−h), each extending the selected chain (height +1), but the deposit is still in the chain at height b. The adversary mines privately at rate h, increasing private height (starting at b−1). Sub-events:
    - If an honest block arrives: h_sel += 1. If h_sel ≥ b + M_dep → deposit matures → CUT. End.
    - If an adversary block arrives: priv += 1. If priv > h_sel (strictly longer) → publish: the selected chain becomes the adversary's branch; its height = priv; adversary blocks added = priv − (b−1) (the number of private blocks). The deposit is removed. The attempt ends in reorg. Then the deposit returns to mempool; the next honest block arrival will start a new attempt.
  - After a successful reorg, the adversary has no active attempt; wait for the next honest block (rate 1−h) to include the deposit and start a new attempt.
  - After the cut, stop.

But wait: after a reorg, does the adversary continue mining privately immediately on its own tip? The rule says "cada vez que aparece en la cadena seleccionada un bloque honesto con depósito, empieza a minar en privado desde su padre". So no mining until the next honest block. During the wait, only honest blocks arrive (rate 1−h). The first honest block includes the deposit → new attempt.

Also, during an attempt, when the adversary publishes, the honest blocks that were mined during the attempt (extensions) are replaced. The private branch's height is priv > h_sel; the new selected height = priv.

Now, adversary block count in the selected chain: each successful reorg adds (priv − (b−1)) adversary blocks to the selected chain (replacing the honest blocks from b onward). Wait, the fork point is at height b−1 (the parent of the deposit block). The private branch has blocks at heights b, b+1, ..., priv. So priv − b + 1 = priv − (b−1) adversary blocks. These replace the honest blocks at heights b..h_sel. So the selected chain now has adversary blocks from b to priv.

The adversary block count in selected chain accumulates those. Also, could the adversary's published branch contain honest blocks? No, private branch is all adversary. So selected chain = prefix up to b−1 (mixed) + adversary blocks b..priv.

Then, next attempt: honest block at priv+1 includes deposit; fork point = priv; new private blocks from priv+1.

The "fraction of selected-chain blocks mined by adversary until cut" = (total adversary blocks in selected chain) / (selected chain height at cut). At cut, selected height = b + M_dep (the deposit matured). Denominator = selected height.

Hmm, one nuance: at the cut moment, the selected chain height is b+M_dep. The adversary blocks in it include all published private blocks from previous reorgs plus... none from the current failed attempt (current private blocks are discarded on cut). So count only published.

Also, the "delay of cut" = t at cut.

Baseline: without adversary, the first honest block at height 1 (rate 1) then M_dep more honest blocks → cut at height 1+M_dep; time ~ Gamma(1+M_dep, 1). But with adversary, total block rate is 1 (honest 1−h + adversary h public). Actually the adversary's public blocks count too. So the selected chain grows at rate 1 in the absence of reorgs. Hmm. I'll report T_cut and note baseline mean.

Wait, actually during an attempt, the honest blocks extend the selected chain at rate 1−h, and adversary private blocks don't extend the selected chain. So the selected chain growth is 1−h during attempts, which is slower than 1. That's the delay. After a reorg, the selected height jumps.

Let me write the simulation with exponential draws:
```
function simular_e4(rng, h, M_dep, horizon)
    t = 0.0
    sel_height = 0      # current selected chain height
    adv_selected = 0    # adversary blocks in selected chain
    deposit_b = 0       # height of current deposit block (0 = none pending)
    priv = 0            # private height during attempt
    pending = true      # is there a deposit in mempool waiting to be included?
    while t < horizon
        if deposit_b == 0
            # wait for next honest block to include deposit
            dt = -log(rand(rng))/(1-h)
            t += dt
            if t >= horizon break
            sel_height += 1
            deposit_b = sel_height
            priv = sel_height - 1
            # attempt starts
        else
            # race: next honest or adversary block
            # honest: rate (1-h); adversary: rate h
            dt_h = -log(rand(rng))/(1-h)
            dt_a = -log(rand(rng))/h
            if dt_h < dt_a
                t += dt_h
                if t >= horizon break
                sel_height += 1
                if sel_height >= deposit_b + M_dep
                    # cut
                    return (cut=true, t=t, frac=adv_selected/sel_height)
                end
                # else continue attempt (deposit still immature)
            else
                t += dt_a
                if t >= horizon break
                priv += 1
                if priv > sel_height
                    # publish
                    adv_selected += priv - (deposit_b - 1)
