Now E3 simulator for validation:
```
function e3_replica!(rng, h,k,delta,Fs,r,p,a)
    rate = h/(1+delta)
    ta = sum of k Exp(rate)
    n = floor(ta/r)
    alpha = (1-a)*p
    # count honest blocks in slots 1..n
    cnt = 0; last = 0
    for j in 1:n
        if rand(rng) < alpha
            cnt+=1; last=j
        end
    end
    # FC-1: d>=Fs?
    part = last >= Fs   # online no cambia, fresh sí
    # FC-3: W_H==0
    fc3 = (cnt == 0)
    return (part, fc3, ta, k)
end
```
This loops n times, which can be large if ta large. But a is irrelevant to n. For E3 MC validation, n = ta/r could be large when r small. Hmm, for r=1/60, ta ~ k(1+δ)/h ~ 12/0.1=120 → n=7200. 1e5 replicas × 7200 = 7e8 per point — slow. But we can compute the count via binomial sampling instead of looping: cnt ~ Binomial(n, alpha), and last = the index of the last success, which requires more care. For part = (last ≥ Fs), we need P(no honest block in slots Fs..n) etc. Simulate: if n≥Fs, part = (there's at least one success in slots Fs..n) — simulate via Binomial(n−Fs+1, alpha)>0. If n<Fs, part=false. And fc3 = (cnt==0) via Binomial(n,alpha)==0. So O(1). 

