But wait — I should double-check my per-attempt model against the alternative reading where the adversary's failure probability is much smaller, so the expected delay exceeds 10^4. Let me compute the expected number of attempts analytically. Per attempt, the adversary wins if it gets 2 net ahead before the honest gets M_dep blocks. The honest wins (cut) with probability p_cut. For h=0.9, M_dep=3, p_cut≈0.0257. Expected attempts 1/p_cut≈39, matching the simulation (156 attempts for one replica is on the high side; the mean should be ~39, but the observed first cut at 156 for seed 1; the distribution is geometric with mean 39, so 156 is plausible (p~0.026, (1−p)^155≈0.017)). Expected time per attempt ≈ wait(1/(1−h)=10) + race time. So expected cut time ≈ 39 × ~12 ≈ 470. For h=0.9. So within 10^4. My single-replica run gave 1839 (variance). So yes, cuts within horizon.

For h→1: p_cut for Mdep: P(Mdep-th honest before (Mdep+2)-th adversary)? Let me generalize: cut iff the Mdep-th H occurs before the (Mdep+2)-th A. With P(H)=1−h, P(A)=h. As h→1, p_cut ≈ C(2Mdep+1, Mdep)(1−h)^Mdep. Wait time per attempt ≈ 1/(1−h). Expected time to cut ≈ (1/p_cut)·(1/(1−h)) ≈ C(...)(1−h)^{-(Mdep+1)}. For Mdep=3, ~C(7,3)(1−h)^{-4} = 35(1−h)^{-4}. For h=0.9, 35·10^4 = 3.5e5, which is > 10^4! Hmm, that contradicts my earlier estimate. Let me recompute: expected attempts 1/p_cut = 1/0.0257 = 39. Expected time per attempt = wait + race ≈ 10 + ~2 = 12. Expected cut time ≈ 470. But 35(1−h)^{-4} = 35·10^4 = 3.5e5. Discrepancy: because p_cut ≈ 0.0257 and 1/(1−h)=10, so 1/p_cut · 1/(1−h) = 39×10 = 390. And C(7,3)(1−h)^Mdep · (1−h)^{-(Mdep+1)} = 35 (1−h)^{-1} = 350. Close-ish (the race time adds). Let me redo: p_cut = P(3rd H before 5th A) = Σ_{n=3}^7 C(n−1,2)(1−h)^3 h^{n−3}. For h=0.9: 0.0257. Then expected attempts 38.9. Expected time per attempt ~ wait(10) + race. So ~390-470. But the formula C(7,3)(1−h)^3 · ... I mis-simplified. Expected time = (1/p_cut)·(1/(1−h)) ≈ 389. Yes ~400, not 3.5e5. I made an algebra error. Good.

For h=0.99: p_cut ≈ ? The leading term: the 3rd H occurs at n=3: (0.01)^3=1e-6; n=4: 3e-6·0.99≈3e-6; etc. p_cut ≈ 1e-6+3e-6+6e-6·0.9801+... ≈ ~1e-5·... let me just say p_cut ~ 1e-5. Wait time 100. Expected cut time ~ 1e7 > 10^4. So for h=0.99, censored. For h=0.9, expected ~400 < 10^4. So the transition is somewhere. For h=0.9, cut likely; for h=0.95, maybe. The grid has h up to 0.9. So for the grid, cuts happen.

Hmm, so the correction's expectation is likely wrong for the grid h=0.6,0.9. Unless the correction author intends a different adversary strategy (continuous mining). I'll report the actual results and the analytic expected-delay, and note the discrepancy.

Hmm, but wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the correction intends the adversary to immediately start a new private branch after publishing, *without waiting for the next honest block*, i.e., continuously censor. Then with h>1/2, the deposit never matures. The phrase "cada vez que aparece en la cadena seleccionada un bloque honesto con depósito, empieza a minar" suggests it starts when the deposit appears, which is after an honest block. But after publishing, the adversary's chain is selected, and there's no deposit (it was removed). The next honest block includes it. So there's a gap where the adversary isn't mining. That gap is the wait. If the adversary instead kept mining during the gap, it would have a head start and censor more effectively. But the model as stated doesn't say that.

I'll go with the literal model and report. This is a genuine result: for h>1/2 in the grid, the deposit is not censored within 10^4; the expected delay is finite. I'll also compute the analytic expected delay and the bound for censoring (h→1).

Actually, let me reconsider the possibility that the correction's process is intended differently regarding "el depósito sale de la cadena y vuelve al mempool; si el depósito madura, el corte ocurre. Solo persigue un depósito a la vez." Maybe "solo persigue un depósito a la vez" means the adversary does NOT re-pursue the same deposit; it moves on to the next deposit. But every honest block includes pending deposits, and the deposit that was reorged returns to the mempool, so it's pending again. If the adversary ignores it and waits for a *new* deposit... but any new honest block will include the returned deposit first (FIFO), so it's the same. Hmm.

Alternatively, maybe once reorged, the adversary considers that deposit "censored" and starts fresh with a new deposit, but the new deposit's maturity clock starts when included; the adversary then censors that one too. For h>1/2, it can censor each one, so no deposit ever matures → censored ~1. But our model has the same deposit re-included, which is equivalent. The difference: in the "always censor" model, the adversary would need to censor every deposit; if it ever fails, cut. Same as repeated attempts. So still cut eventually.

Unless the "attempt" starts from the *parent of the deposit block*, and after a reorg the adversary's published branch already contains the parent; the honest then builds on the adversary's tip and includes the deposit. The adversary starts from the deposit's parent = its own tip. But it already has a chain; maybe the adversary is now "ahead" by construction and can orphan immediately? No.

OK. Let me proceed with the literal model and report.

Actually, hold on. Let me reconsider the maturity rule once more: "Un depósito incluido en el bloque de altura b madura cuando la cadena seleccionada alcanza altura b + M_dep con ese bloque dentro." So the deposit at b matures when the selected chain reaches height b+M_dep *with that block inside*. If the adversary reorgs, the deposit block is out; the deposit returns to mempool and is included at some height b'. Its clock restarts. So yes.

